  "salt": "hujIUYXavPqa1Ftnru6oRw==",
  "text": "The Welsh tale of Kulhwch and Olwen tells of the knight Kay. Kay could go days without sleep and stay underwater as long. He could lengthen his body to the top of the tallest tree. His body heat warmed his friends in the cold. A pack on his back turned invisible. In a fight a hundred or more of the enemy fell at his blade. Kay also appears in the tale of the queen Guinevere. The king Meleagant carried her off. Kay and Lancelot tracked him to his castle. Kay was hurt trying to free the queen. Lancelot slipped into the room where Kay lay asleep. Blood on the sheets next day pointed at Kay. Lancelot fought to clear his friend's name. Some scholars hold that Kay began as a god of Celtic peoples. These were the old foes of Arthur's tribes. The tie between the two heroes reads as a folk memory of real politics.",
